"Poor ventilation systems in modern office buildings are recycling old, stale air and spreading airborne infections. Various studies have found instances where tuberculosis, measles, and flu were spread throughout sealed, modern office buildings that added little or no fresh air. Other places where infections spread rapidly include airplanes, pediatricians' offices, prisons, hospitals, and homeless shelters. Adding 15, or even less than 10, cubic feet per minute of outdoor air has become common. Adding even 35 cubic feet per minute as recommended in Massachusetts would significantly decrease infection's spread, but can be expensive.","What's the link? A poor ventilation system that does little more than recycle old, stale air from one end of the building to another.; Millions of American office workers are getting more than they bargained for when they inhale in a building that has no fresh air. Along with oxygen, they may be getting flu and other infectious bugs from their co-workers.; That's because, in today's modern office buildings, recirculated air can mean recycled flu and other airborne infections. The evidence comes from various studies of tuberculosis, measles and flu that spread in a manner that can only be explained by poor air ventilation.; ""Modern office buildings are tight,"" says Jon Rosenberg, public health officer in California's occupational health program. ""In order to conserve energy, they're often sealed.""; Experts say the problem is not the same as ""sick building syndrome"" -- a situation in which workers are exposed chronically to toxic chemicals and gases emitted from carpets, furniture and other building materials. Nor is it to blame for last month's outbreak in Richmond of Legionnaire's disease -- an illness caused by a bug linked to wet, damp spaces rather than poor ventilation.; But as",1
