"So far this season, 31,000 brush and timber fires have been reported in seven Western states and Alaska and Georgia. 1,111,000 acres have burned so far compared to 765,000 acres last season. In the West, the fires were fought in Utah's Diamond Park and Vinta Canyon; Northern California's Sierra foothills; Colorado's drought-stricken mountains and eastern plains; Wyoming's Bridger-Teton National Forest; Arizona's Mount Graham, Peloncillo and Rincon Mountains areas; and New Mexico's Gila National Forest. Four large fires burned in interior Alaska, one burned in Georgia's Okeefeenokee Swamp, and another in north central Washington. Firefighters have contended with wind and high temperatures.","Thousands more acres of brush and timber went up in smoke Saturday in seven states in the West, threatening homes in some places, and firefighters contended with wind and high temperatures. ""As the day heats up, you'll get these reburns going out and the trees dry out and they'll torch,"" said Forest Service spokesman Ed Christian in Wyoming. ""We hope Mother Nature cooperates with us,"" said Mary Plumb of the federal Bureau of Land Management in Utah. Record highs included 97 at Cheyenne, Wyo., and 100 at Denver, while Casper, Wyo., tied its record of 100. That was Denver's fifth consecutive day at 100 degrees or higher. Fire crews were at work in Utah, Wyoming, Colorado, New Mexico, Arizona, California and Washington. Elsewhere, four big fires burned in interior Alaska, one having charred an estimated 24,000 acres. A fire in Georgia's Okefenoke swamp that burned 500 acres of habitat for an endangered species of woodpecker was reported contained Saturday. The Boise Interagency Fire Center in Idaho, which coordinates federal firefighting efforts, listed 7,000 to 7,500 people on the fire lines, along with 42 air tankers, 14 guide planes and 10 helicopters, spokesman Arnold Hartigan sai",1
