"On Monday Slovenia's parliament declared the republic's full sovereignty, but stopped short of calling for secession. A coalition of center-right parties formed a new government in Slovenia last month after overwhelming reform Communists in the first free elections held in Communist-ruled Yugoslavia in 45 years. The new government demands that Yugoslavia transform itself into a loose confederation of sovereign states and threatens to declare full independence if the remaining republics do not agree. The hard-line Communist government in the largest republic of Serbia vehemently opposes turning Yugoslavia into a confederation and has made no plans for free elections.","The lawmakers who replaced the Communists as leaders of the prosperous northern republic of Slovenia have proclaimed the state's full sovereignty, but stopped short of calling for secession. There was no immediate reaction to the Slovenian declaration from federal authorities or from rival Serbia. Monday's parliamentary declaration provides for establishing an independent legal system that would take precedence over federal laws and for Slovenian control over armed forces stationed in the republic, state media said. The document said Slovenia should adopt a new democratic constitution within the next 12 months, but made no mention of earlier calls for the republic to secede from the troubled Yugoslav federation. The media said a joint session of the republic's 240-seat legislature voted unanimously to adopt the Declaration of Sovereignty of the State of Slovenia. Slovenia contains 2 million of Yugoslavia's total population of 23 million. The Ljubljana nightly television news said the declaration asked Slovenian authorities to ""assume control over units of the (Yugoslav) armed forces stationed on Slovenian territory,"" and said a 30 percent cut in defense spending would be implemente",1
