"Sites of potential marathon-centered vacations include Bangkok (November), Belgrade (held despite Yugoslavia's turmoil), Berlin (Sept 27th, grown with the wall's demise), Bermuda, Brussels ($16 entry fee), Budapest (offering a half-marathon and mini- marathon along the Danube), Iceland, London (April 12th; the world's largest marathon, with restricted entry but no qualifying times), Moscow, Munich (repeating the 1972 Olympic route and finishing in the same stadium), Paris, Portland (including a five-mile run, five-mile walk, and a kids run), Rome, Seoul, Stockholm (May), Tel Aviv (passing houses hit by Gulf War Scud missiles, with a half-marathon and fun run), Venice ($40), and Vienna.","But a unique subset has formed: people who like to do both at the same time. And to help these folks along, companies arrange tours for marathon vacations. The result? Bangkok in November, London in April and Stockholm in May.; ""Skiers have mountain ranges, tennis players have resorts, why can't a runner take a marathon vacation?"" said Thorn Gilligan, president of Marathon Tours in Boston. ""A runner should be able to enjoy a closed city for a day. It's a celebration for the sport as much as a competition.""; Gilligan took 5,500 people last year on 20 tours to countries from Bermuda to Moscow. In addition to air fare and hotel, runners are guaranteed spots in restricted entry races such as the London Marathon. Qualifying times are not required.; London, the most popular European city to visit, holds the world's largest marathon (April 12 next year). Berlin's race (Sept. 27, 1992) has grown with the demise of the wall.; ""Most marathons take you through cities, so you get a chance to see things you'd never see on a sightseeing bus,"" said Fred Lebow, president of the New York Roadrunner's Club and director of the New York Marathon. Lebow has run in 32 marathons, including London, Paris,",1
"On July 1, 1991 President Bush nominated Clarence Thomas, a black conservative republican, to replace Thurgood Marshall on the Supreme Court. Thomas is a bootstrap conservative who does not believe in affirmative action, quotas or racial preferences for minority groups, saying that these are unconstitutional. Thomas studied for the priesthood at one time. He has never taken a stand on abortion, which Catholicism opposes. Abortion rights proponents intend to oppose him. His stand on the major issues is largely unknown. President Bush believes that Thomas is a self- made success. If confirmed, he could be on the Supreme Court by October, 1991.","But Thomas, who has risen in Republican ranks as an advocate of bootstrap conservatism, would present a striking change from Marshall, a civil-rights pioneer and an anchor of the court's declining liberal faction.; Thomas, barely half the age of the man whose seat he was named to fill, came of age in the world that Marshall helped create.; Now the question is to what extent Thomas would add weight to the court's new majority that appears willing to dismantle important parts of the legacy that Marshall helped to build.; While not much is known about Thomas' views on most issues likely to come before the court, he has made clear his opposition to affirmative action and to policies that can be viewed as incorporating quotas or racial preferences.; Opposes affirmative action; In his year as a federal appeals court judge, he has not ruled on affirmative action, but he has long been outspoken in taking the position that government affirmative-action programs are not only unwise but unconstitutional.; However, in other respects, this nomination is very much a wild card both for conservatives who jumped instantly to embrace it and for liberals whose responses ranged from cautious to hostil",1
