"A Wednesday tornado, one of a series in an arc spanning 7 states, hopped a 25-mile path and carved a 3-mile stretch through Huntsville, plowing through a school, shopping mall, apartments and rural areas. It killed 17, injured 463 and left 1,000 homeless. Most dead were in apartments, stores or cars. Wind, rain, and temperatures plummeting to the 30s hampered the search for victims. A tornado watch was issued earlier in the day but the tornado warning did not come until 4:39pm, when the twister was already tearing up the city, sounding like a jet at close range and bringing a deluge of water.","Rescuers crawled through collapsed homes and shops today looking for more victims of a tornado that carved a 3-mile stretch of destruction, killing 17 people, injuring 463 and leaving 1,000 homeless. ""It's like taking six to 10 city blocks and putting them in a blender and putting it on liquefy,"" said rescue worker Bob Caraway, whose specialty is cave rescues. He was among those called out to help dig through rubble for survivors or the bodies of the dead. The tornado was one of a series that touched down Wednesday in an arc spanning at least seven states from the Deep South to the Midwest. The other tornadoes caused at least 19 injuries and far-flung property damage. In Huntsville, teams with cranes and floodlights searched for the injured or dead, hampered by wind-whipped rain and temperatures that plummeted overnight from 73 degrees into the 30s. Gov. Guy Hunt sent 50 National Guardsmen to help and said he would view damage Friday. His spokesman, Terry Abbott, said aerial surveys indicate the twister hopped along a 25-mile path, much of it straight through Huntsville. By this afternoon, severe thunderstorms were crossing the Northeast. The National Weather Service put out a torn",1
